    "case_id": "63",
    "case_summary": "A 48-year-old male with no prior health issues reported to the surgical outpatient service with a 3-month history of increasing difficulty swallowing solid foods, accompanied by regurgitation and an unintentional weight loss of 14 kg. Physical examination did not reveal any abnormalities, and there were no signs of enlarged organs or swollen lymph nodes.\n\nSubsequent oesophagoscopy revealed a large polypoid pigmented lesion at 30 cm. Multiple biopsies were taken. A staging CT scan of his thorax, abdomen and pelvis showed a well-defined eccentric mass in the mid-lower oesophagus. Whole body FDG PET/CT body scan confirmed the mass in the oesophagus with increased uptake in a high abdominal pre-aortic and high right paratracheal node consistent with metastasis. Biopsy results from the oesophagoscopy showed an infiltrating malignant tumour with prominent nucleoli and cells prominent in the submucosa and also in the basal layer of the squamous mucosa. S100 stain was positive. The features that confirmed the primary nature of the neoplasm were the junctional change, multi-pleomorphic spindle shaped cells with prominent nucleoli and some lymphocytic infiltrate.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
